WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.772
Every dependency becomes part of a
service's security and reliability story.

2
00:00:03.852 --> 00:00:06.778
In Go services, dependencies may include
Go modules, generated code,

3
00:00:06.858 --> 00:00:10.080
container base images, build tools, CI
actions, operating system packages,

4
00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:11.102
and runtime sidecars.

5
00:00:11.182 --> 00:00:13.265
Each one can affect what code runs, how it
is built,

6
00:00:13.345 --> 00:00:14.733
and how it behaves in production.

7
00:00:14.813 --> 00:00:18.432
Go modules and version information help
teams make dependency decisions repeatable.

8
00:00:18.512 --> 00:00:21.289
They are valuable, but they do not replace
review and maintenance.

9
00:00:21.369 --> 00:00:23.203
A pinned version can still contain a
defect,

10
00:00:23.283 --> 00:00:25.366
and an updated version can still change
behavior.

11
00:00:25.446 --> 00:00:29.114
Defensive architecture treats dependency
updates as controlled engineering changes

12
00:00:29.194 --> 00:00:30.087
with visible review.

13
00:00:30.167 --> 00:00:33.836
Prefer small, well-understood dependency
sets over unnecessary packages for routine

14
00:00:33.916 --> 00:00:34.213
tasks.

15
00:00:34.293 --> 00:00:36.326
This does not mean avoiding libraries
entirely.

16
00:00:36.406 --> 00:00:39.232
It means considering whether a package is
needed, who maintains it,

17
00:00:39.312 --> 00:00:41.940
how widely it is used, what transitive
dependencies it brings,

18
00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:44.896
and whether the standard library already
provides a clear solution.

19
00:00:44.976 --> 00:00:47.702
Build and release architecture should make
it clear what source,

20
00:00:47.782 --> 00:00:50.261
version, configuration, and artifact are
being deployed.

21
00:00:50.341 --> 00:00:52.969
Teams should be able to trace a running
service back to a commit,

22
00:00:53.049 --> 00:00:55.727
build pipeline, module set, image, and
configuration profile.

23
00:00:55.807 --> 00:00:59.426
Repeatability matters because incident
response and rollback depend on knowing what

24
00:00:59.506 --> 00:00:59.903
changed.

25
00:00:59.983 --> 00:01:03.255
Container images and runtime packages
should be kept minimal and maintained.

26
00:01:03.335 --> 00:01:06.657
A smaller runtime surface can reduce
operational complexity and make scanning

27
00:01:06.737 --> 00:01:07.778
results easier to act on.

28
00:01:07.858 --> 00:01:09.692
Base images, certificates, time zone data,

29
00:01:09.772 --> 00:01:13.243
and system packages should have an
ownership and update path, not a one-time setup.

30
00:01:13.323 --> 00:01:16.645
Defensive supply-chain architecture
focuses on visibility, repeatability,

31
00:01:16.725 --> 00:01:18.063
review, and controlled change.

32
00:01:18.143 --> 00:01:20.028
The goal is not to freeze the service
forever.

33
00:01:20.108 --> 00:01:22.339
The goal is to make changes intentional,
observable,

34
00:01:22.419 --> 00:01:25.939
and reversible enough that teams can
improve security without losing confidence in

35
00:01:26.019 --> 00:01:27.060
what they are deploying.
